1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,389
June thirtieth, nineteen oh eight.

2
00:00:02,609 --> 00:00:04,464
Just after seven in the morning.

3
00:00:04,684 --> 00:00:10,360
Over a remote forest in Siberia, something brighter than the sun streaks across the sky.

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:12,094
Then it explodes.

5
00:00:12,314 --> 00:00:13,114
In the air.

6
00:00:13,614 --> 00:00:17,369
The blast flattens an area of forest bigger than New York City.

7
00:00:17,589 --> 00:00:20,360
Windows break more than sixty kilometers away.

8
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:23,005
Instruments in Britain feel the shock wave.

9
00:00:23,505 --> 00:00:28,638
And when scientists finally get there, almost twenty years later, there is no crater.

10
00:00:28,858 --> 00:00:30,015
No giant rock.

11
00:00:30,235 --> 00:00:30,914
Nothing.

12
00:00:31,134 --> 00:00:37,514
Just millions of trees lying down like someone pressed a giant snooze button on the entire forest.

13
00:00:38,914 --> 00:00:44,316
Hi, I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today's file is the Tunguska event.

14
00:00:44,536 --> 00:00:46,923
The biggest impact in recorded history.

15
00:00:47,143 --> 00:00:49,042
That never actually hit the ground.

16
00:00:49,542 --> 00:00:50,689
Fun timing, too.

17
00:00:50,909 --> 00:00:58,805
This month, two of the biggest asteroids in the solar system, Vesta and Pallas, are bright enough to spot with binoculars.

18
00:00:59,025 --> 00:01:05,775
So if you're looking up anyway, this is a good day to learn what happens when one of their smaller cousins drops by.

19
00:01:06,275 --> 00:01:09,797
We'll follow the eyewitnesses, the scientists, and the theories.

20
00:01:10,017 --> 00:01:12,617
Including a few that are completely bonkers.

21
00:01:12,837 --> 00:01:16,168
And I'll tell you which ones the evidence actually supports.

22
00:01:17,568 --> 00:01:18,915
First, where this happened.

23
00:01:19,135 --> 00:01:25,309
The Podkamennaya Tunguska River, in central Siberia, in what was then the Russian Empire.

24
00:01:25,809 --> 00:01:27,078
This is deep taiga.

25
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:32,080
Endless pine and larch forest, swamps, rivers, and almost no people.

26
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:40,307
The nearest settlement worth a dot on the map was a small trading post called Vanavara, about sixty five kilometers away.

27
00:01:40,807 --> 00:01:47,201
If you were picking a spot on Earth to drop a giant explosion, this is honestly the most polite choice.

28
00:01:47,421 --> 00:01:49,422
Imagine the same thing over Paris.

29
00:01:49,642 --> 00:01:50,823
Or over your house.

30
00:01:51,323 --> 00:01:52,235
Remember that.

31
00:01:52,455 --> 00:01:57,001
Because it's the reason this story is a mystery instead of a catastrophe.

32
00:01:58,401 --> 00:02:05,161
The people closest to the blast were the Evenki, reindeer herders who lived and traveled across this forest.

33
00:02:05,661 --> 00:02:08,678
Some of their camps were much closer than Vanavara.

34
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:15,621
Their accounts, collected years later, describe tents blown over, reindeer killed, and people thrown through the air.

35
00:02:16,121 --> 00:02:18,725
At least three people are reported to have died.

36
00:02:18,945 --> 00:02:23,121
In such an empty place, it's possible that's close to the real number.

37
00:02:23,341 --> 00:02:25,642
It's also possible we'll never know.

38
00:02:27,042 --> 00:02:28,873
Now, the most famous eyewitness.

39
00:02:29,093 --> 00:02:33,874
A man named Semenov, sitting on the porch of the trading post at Vanavara.

40
00:02:34,374 --> 00:02:39,356
He described the sky splitting in two, and fire appearing high above the forest.

41
00:02:39,576 --> 00:02:43,587
Then a wave of heat so strong he felt his shirt was about to burn.

42
00:02:44,087 --> 00:02:45,317
Then the shock wave hit.

43
00:02:45,537 --> 00:02:51,197
It threw him to the ground, several meters from the porch, and he briefly lost consciousness.

44
00:02:51,697 --> 00:02:53,567
Sixty five kilometers away.

45
00:02:53,787 --> 00:02:56,683
That's roughly the distance from Manhattan to Princeton.

46
00:02:56,903 --> 00:03:01,171
Imagine getting knocked off your porch by something happening in New Jersey.

47
00:03:02,571 --> 00:03:03,721
So how big was it?

48
00:03:03,941 --> 00:03:09,105
Estimates have bounced around a lot over the years, from about three to thirty megatons.

49
00:03:09,325 --> 00:03:11,559
Ten to fifteen is a common figure.

50
00:03:12,059 --> 00:03:17,193
For scale, the atomic bomb dropped on Hiroshima was about fifteen kilotons.

51
00:03:17,413 --> 00:03:21,456
So Tunguska was somewhere around a thousand times more powerful.

52
00:03:21,956 --> 00:03:24,908
And here's the part that makes scientists' eyes light up.

53
00:03:25,128 --> 00:03:30,085
All of that energy was released in the air, several kilometers above the ground.

54
00:03:30,305 --> 00:03:31,423
Not on impact.

55
00:03:31,923 --> 00:03:38,439
It's the universe's version of a really dramatic entrance where the guest explodes before reaching the door.

56
00:03:39,839 --> 00:03:42,116
The explosion didn't just shake Siberia.

57
00:03:42,336 --> 00:03:44,097
Seismic stations recorded it.

58
00:03:44,317 --> 00:03:49,191
And the pressure wave in the air was picked up by instruments as far away as Britain.

59
00:03:49,691 --> 00:03:52,129
Nobody knew what they were looking at, of course.

60
00:03:52,349 --> 00:03:59,695
In nineteen oh eight there were no satellites, no internet, and Siberia was very far from everyone with a newspaper.

61
00:04:00,195 --> 00:04:06,109
The scientists in Europe saw a strange wiggle on their charts, shrugged, and went back to their tea.

62
00:04:07,509 --> 00:04:09,213
But people did notice something.

63
00:04:09,433 --> 00:04:14,868
For several nights after the explosion, the skies over Europe and western Russia glowed.

64
00:04:15,368 --> 00:04:18,855
People reported nights so bright you could read outside.

65
00:04:19,075 --> 00:04:20,682
Some photographed the sky.

66
00:04:20,902 --> 00:04:23,027
Some thought it was the end of the world.

67
00:04:23,247 --> 00:04:24,683
Some just stayed out late.

68
00:04:25,183 --> 00:04:31,591
Most likely, dust and ice thrown high into the atmosphere were catching sunlight from below the horizon.

69
00:04:31,811 --> 00:04:37,220
Basically, Earth got a cosmic night light, and nobody had any idea where it came from.

70
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:40,137
And then, for years, nothing.

71
00:04:40,357 --> 00:04:41,412
World War One.

72
00:04:41,632 --> 00:04:42,996
The Russian Revolution.

73
00:04:43,216 --> 00:04:44,165
A civil war.

74
00:04:44,385 --> 00:04:48,408
Nobody had time to go look for a mysterious explosion in a swamp.

75
00:04:48,908 --> 00:04:54,881
Finally, in the nineteen twenties, a mineralogist named Leonid Kulik became obsessed with it.

76
00:04:55,101 --> 00:05:01,391
He was convinced a giant meteorite had hit Siberia, and that it might be full of valuable iron.

77
00:05:01,891 --> 00:05:06,111
His first expedition, in nineteen twenty one, didn't even reach the site.

78
00:05:06,331 --> 00:05:12,432
In nineteen twenty seven he finally made it, hiking through bogs, mosquitoes and fallen forest.

79
00:05:12,932 --> 00:05:23,000
Picture spending years planning a treasure hunt, crossing half of Siberia, getting eaten alive by mosquitoes, and finding out the treasure might not exist.

80
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:24,739
That's not an expedition.

81
00:05:24,959 --> 00:05:26,309
That's a group project.

82
00:05:27,709 --> 00:05:29,400
What Kulik found was staggering.

83
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:35,643
Trees knocked flat across an area of about two thousand one hundred and fifty square kilometers.

84
00:05:36,143 --> 00:05:38,856
You'll often hear the number eighty million trees.

85
00:05:39,076 --> 00:05:41,886
That's a popular estimate, not a careful count.

86
00:05:42,106 --> 00:05:43,500
But it gives you the idea.

87
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:45,583
A whole forest, lying down.

88
00:05:46,083 --> 00:05:47,652
And they weren't lying randomly.

89
00:05:47,872 --> 00:05:54,122
They pointed outward, away from one central area, like a giant had pushed them over from the middle.

90
00:05:55,522 --> 00:05:58,520
So Kulik went to the center, expecting a crater.

91
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:00,031
And instead found this.

92
00:06:00,531 --> 00:06:01,820
Trees still standing.

93
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:04,652
Stripped of their branches and bark, but upright.

94
00:06:05,152 --> 00:06:06,877
That's actually a huge clue.

95
00:06:07,097 --> 00:06:14,316
Directly under an explosion in the air, the blast comes straight down, so the trees get stripped but not knocked over.

96
00:06:14,536 --> 00:06:17,859
Further out, the blast comes in at an angle and flattens them.

97
00:06:18,359 --> 00:06:21,742
Kulik kept searching for his giant meteorite for years.

98
00:06:21,962 --> 00:06:23,096
He drained swamps.

99
00:06:23,316 --> 00:06:24,356
He dug holes.

100
00:06:24,576 --> 00:06:25,656
He never found it.

101
00:06:25,876 --> 00:06:27,761
And that's not even the strangest part.

102
00:06:27,981 --> 00:06:33,302
The strangest part is how many people later decided they knew exactly what it was.

103
00:06:34,702 --> 00:06:36,232
Let's go through the theories.

104
00:06:36,452 --> 00:06:39,201
Theory one, the one most scientists accept.

105
00:06:39,421 --> 00:06:46,033
A stony asteroid, maybe fifty to sixty meters across, hit the atmosphere at tremendous speed.

106
00:06:46,533 --> 00:06:54,481
The air in front of it compressed and heated so much that the rock broke apart and essentially exploded a few kilometers up.

107
00:06:54,701 --> 00:06:55,580
An airburst.

108
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:59,089
No crater, because nothing big enough reached the ground.

109
00:06:59,589 --> 00:07:00,510
Theory two.

110
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:01,463
A comet.

111
00:07:01,683 --> 00:07:07,887
Comets are mostly ice and dust, so they could explode completely and leave almost nothing behind.

112
00:07:08,107 --> 00:07:10,645
That would also help explain the glowing nights.

113
00:07:11,145 --> 00:07:12,099
Theory three.

114
00:07:12,319 --> 00:07:16,070
A small lake about eight kilometers away, called Lake Cheko.

115
00:07:16,290 --> 00:07:21,617
Some Italian researchers proposed it might be a crater made by a surviving fragment.

116
00:07:21,837 --> 00:07:24,946
Others think the lake is older than nineteen oh eight.

117
00:07:25,166 --> 00:07:26,711
That debate is still going.

118
00:07:28,111 --> 00:07:29,553
And now, the fun section.

119
00:07:29,773 --> 00:07:32,452
The theories that are, let's say, enthusiastic.

120
00:07:32,952 --> 00:07:38,606
Some people claimed it was a test of a secret death ray by the inventor Nikola Tesla.

121
00:07:38,826 --> 00:07:40,517
There is no evidence for this.

122
00:07:40,737 --> 00:07:46,096
It's a story that sounds cool on the internet, which is not the same thing as being true.

123
00:07:46,596 --> 00:07:52,207
Others suggested a tiny black hole passing through the Earth, or a crashed alien spaceship.

124
00:07:52,427 --> 00:07:57,477
These ideas have appeared mostly in fiction and speculation, not in evidence.

125
00:07:57,977 --> 00:07:59,536
Look, I love a good alien.

126
00:07:59,756 --> 00:08:11,859
But when you have a giant explosion in the sky, a flattened forest pointing away from one spot, and a planet that gets hit by space rocks all the time, the boring answer is probably right.

127
00:08:12,079 --> 00:08:13,203
Sorry, aliens.

128
00:08:14,603 --> 00:08:16,768
And here's why Tunguska still matters.

129
00:08:16,988 --> 00:08:22,889
In twenty thirteen, a much smaller asteroid exploded over the Russian city of Chelyabinsk.

130
00:08:23,389 --> 00:08:25,317
It was caught on dashcams everywhere.

131
00:08:25,537 --> 00:08:31,811
The shock wave blew out windows and injured more than a thousand people, mostly from flying glass.

132
00:08:32,311 --> 00:08:34,729
That was a rock around twenty meters wide.

133
00:08:34,949 --> 00:08:39,858
Tunguska was probably two or three times wider, which means far more energy.

134
00:08:40,358 --> 00:08:47,536
Tunguska is the reason the United Nations now marks June thirtieth as International Asteroid Day.

135
00:08:47,756 --> 00:08:50,035
It's a reminder to keep watching the sky.

136
00:08:50,255 --> 00:08:52,284
And to fund the people doing the watching.

137
00:08:53,684 --> 00:08:55,410
So here's the honest version.

138
00:08:55,910 --> 00:08:56,847
Documented.

139
00:08:57,067 --> 00:09:07,547
On June thirtieth, nineteen oh eight, a huge explosion over central Siberia flattened about two thousand one hundred and fifty square kilometers of forest.

140
00:09:07,767 --> 00:09:10,052
It was recorded by instruments far away.

141
00:09:10,272 --> 00:09:12,948
Skies over Europe glowed for nights afterward.

142
00:09:13,168 --> 00:09:16,645
Eyewitnesses were knocked down sixty five kilometers away.

143
00:09:16,865 --> 00:09:19,469
At least three people are reported to have died.

144
00:09:19,689 --> 00:09:21,530
No crater has been confirmed.

145
00:09:22,030 --> 00:09:22,888
Disputed.

146
00:09:23,108 --> 00:09:30,835
The exact energy, the size of the object, whether it was rocky or icy, and whether Lake Cheko is part of the story.

147
00:09:31,335 --> 00:09:32,065
Legend.

148
00:09:32,285 --> 00:09:38,475
The Tesla death ray, the alien ship, the black hole, and the exact figure of eighty million trees.

149
00:09:39,875 --> 00:09:40,987
So, your verdict.

150
00:09:41,207 --> 00:09:44,256
Asteroid, comet, or are you still team aliens?

151
00:09:44,476 --> 00:09:45,481
I won't judge.

152
00:09:45,701 --> 00:09:46,296
Much.

153
00:09:46,796 --> 00:09:48,040
Tell me in the comments.

154
00:09:48,260 --> 00:09:53,233
And if a giant fireball ever appears in the sky, please don't stand by the window.

155
00:09:53,453 --> 00:09:56,269
That's the one lesson Chelyabinsk taught everyone.

156
00:09:56,769 --> 00:10:00,847
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

157
00:10:01,067 --> 00:10:05,450
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
